To include the `ABOR` and `TYPE` commands in the proper locations within the original sequence of client requests, we can consider the logical flow of FTP commands. The `TYPE` command is typically sent after the initial login commands and before any data transfer commands (like `LIST`), while `ABOR` can be sent at any point to abort the current operation.

Here’s how the modified sequence of client requests would look:

```
USER anonymous
PASS ubuntu
SYST
PWD
TYPE I           # Set the transfer type to binary (or ASCII, depending on the requirement)
PORT 127,0,0,1,152,193
LIST
ABOR            # This can be added here if you want to ensure that any previous LIST command is aborted
QUIT
```

If you want to keep it simple and just add `TYPE` without `ABOR`, it would look like this:

```
USER anonymous
PASS ubuntu
SYST
PWD
TYPE I           # Set the transfer type to binary (or ASCII, depending on the requirement)
PORT 127,0,0,1,152,193
LIST
QUIT
```

Feel free to adjust the `TYPE` command depending on whether you want binary (`TYPE I`) or ASCII (`TYPE A`) transfer. If you want to include `ABOR`, it can be placed after `LIST` or anywhere appropriate in the flow

